<div class="cover-top">
  <div class="meta">REINFORCEMENT-LEARNING PROJECT REPORT &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; INGOLSTADT MORNING RUSH HOUR &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 25 OCTOBER 2026</div>
  <h1>CityMind AI</h1>
  <div class="sub">One neural network that runs 98 traffic lights - and does better than the simulator's own signal logic</div>
</div>

<div class="box plain"><span class="t">The project in 60 seconds</span>
<ol>
<li><b>Problem.</b> Every traffic light needs a rule for when to turn green. We simulate the morning rush hour (07:00-08:00, about 15,000 cars, 98 signalised junctions) of the German city Ingolstadt in the traffic simulator SUMO. The simulator's own rule - "actuated" control, which keeps a green on while cars keep arriving - is the opponent we must beat.</li>
<li><b>Idea.</b> Let a computer <i>learn</i> the rule by trial and error (reinforcement learning). We train <b>one</b> neural network and copy it to all 98 junctions. Every 5 simulated seconds each junction shows the network 22 numbers about its traffic and gets back one answer: <i>stay green</i> or <i>move to the next green</i>.</li>
<li><b>Learning.</b> The network is rewarded for fewer waiting cars. It practised on about 11 million such decisions using the PPO algorithm (book Ch.11), with a few project-specific fixes that made the difference between "learns nothing" and "wins".</li>
<li><b>Result.</b> Tested on 10 random traffic seeds against the baseline on identical traffic, both trained models are better on 12 / 11 of 14 measures and worse on none. Cars wait about <b>41 % less</b> and about <b>200-260 more trips finish per hour</b>.</li>
</ol></div>

