<!-- ===================================================== 2 PROBLEM ===================================================== -->
<div>
<h2>2 &nbsp;The problem and the opponent</h2>
<h3>The scenario</h3>
<p>We use <b>InTAS</b>, a published, realistic model of Ingolstadt's road network and traffic, inside the open-source simulator <b>Eclipse SUMO</b>. We simulate one morning rush hour, 07:00-08:00, in which about 15,000 cars are scheduled to drive. <b>98 junctions</b> have traffic lights. The simulator moves every car second by second, so a bad signal rule shows up as queues, slow trips and cars that cannot even enter the network because the entry road is jammed.</p>
<h3>The opponent: SUMO "actuated" control</h3>
<p>76 of the 98 signal programs in the scenario are <i>vehicle-actuated</i>: a detector sees cars, the green is extended while cars keep coming and ends at a gap, always between a minimum and a maximum duration. This is a good, adaptive rule - far better than a plain timer. We confirmed it: a pure fixed-time plan is <b>worse</b> than actuated control on 11 of the 14 measures (lag 298.7 s vs 276.8 s), so actuated control is the real bar.</p>
<div class="box code"><span class="t">Where in the code</span>
<p>Baseline controllers: {{c:src/controllers/sumo_actuated.py:1}} (lets SUMO run its own programs untouched), {{c:src/controllers/fixed_time.py:1}} (plain timer, for context). Trained agent: {{c:src/evaluation/evaluate.py:257}} (<code>_run_ippo_episode</code>). All three are scored by the same code.</p></div>
<h3>What "better" means - the goal in numbers</h3>
<p>We do not want a model that looks good only on one number. We therefore score <b>14 measures</b> (section 10), chosen so that the model cannot win by cheating - for example, by blocking cars from entering the network (that would shorten queues inside but leave cars waiting outside). The main number is <b>lag per scheduled car</b>: for every car that was <i>supposed</i> to drive in this hour, how many seconds later than on an empty road did it finish? Cars that never finish or never enter count as "late until 08:00". Section 9 gives the formula.</p>
</div>

<!-- ===================================================== 3 RL FRAMING ===================================================== -->
<div>
<h2>3 &nbsp;Reinforcement learning, in our words</h2>
<div class="box book"><span class="t">What the book says</span>
<p>RL is learning by trial and error: an <b>agent</b> acts in an <b>environment</b>, sees the new <b>state</b> and gets a <b>reward</b>; its goal is to maximise the total reward ({{b:Ch.1 p.6-11}}). The agent's behaviour is its <b>policy</b>; the <b>value function</b> says how good a state is under that policy ({{b:Ch.1 p.9-10}}, {{b:Ch.3 p.45-46}}). The problem is formalised as a <b>Markov decision process</b> ({{b:Ch.3 p.43}}).</p></div>

<div class="fig"><img src="fig/fig_loop.png" width="86%"><div class="cap">Figure 1 - The agent-environment loop of {{b:Ch.1 p.11}}, with our real quantities. One pass = 5 simulated seconds; one hour = 720 passes.</div></div>

<table>
<tr><th style="width:17%">RL word</th><th style="width:44%">In CityMind</th><th>Code</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Environment</b></td><td>SUMO simulating Ingolstadt; one agent slot per traffic light (98), wrapped in the standard multi-agent interface PettingZoo.</td><td>{{c:src/environment/decentralized_sumo_env.py:43}}</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Agent</b></td><td>One neural network that is <i>copied</i> to every junction - all junctions share the same weights.</td><td>{{c:src/agents/ippo_agent.py:151}}</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>State</b> s</td><td>22 numbers describing one junction and its neighbours (section 4).</td><td>{{c:src/environment/local_observation.py:151}}</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Action</b> a</td><td>Two choices, every 5 s: <code>0</code> keep the current green, <code>1</code> advance to the next green.</td><td>{{c:src/environment/safety.py:106}}</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Reward</b> r</td><td>Minus (weighted) cars waiting - always &le; 0, so "less negative" = better (section 6).</td><td>{{c:src/environment/local_reward.py:85}}</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Policy</b> &pi;(a|s)</td><td>The actor network: 22 inputs &rarr; 256 &rarr; 256 &rarr; probability of "advance".</td><td>{{c:src/agents/ippo_agent.py:221}}</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Value</b> V(s)</td><td>The critic network (same shape): predicts how much waiting is still to come from this state.</td><td>{{c:src/agents/ippo_agent.py:221}}</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Episode</b></td><td>One simulated hour = 720 decisions per junction.</td><td>{{c:configs/environment.yaml:5}}</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Why it is a hard version of RL</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Partially observable.</b> The book's Markov property says the future depends only on the present state ({{b:Ch.3 p.41}}). A junction cannot see the whole city, only its own lanes and a few neighbours - this is the "partially observable environment" of {{b:Ch.1 p.13}}. So we design the 22 numbers carefully to include what matters (section 4).</li>
<li><b>Many agents at once.</b> 98 junctions act simultaneously and affect each other (a queue released upstream arrives downstream). The book names multi-agent environments among the environment types, as mostly stochastic ({{b:Ch.1 p.13}}). Our answer is <i>independent learners with shared weights</i> (IPPO, section 7).</li>
<li><b>Delayed consequences.</b> A green given now empties a queue over the next minute; a green given to the wrong road is felt only later. That is the reason for the discount factor below.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Return and the discount factor</h3>
<p>The <b>return</b> is the total future reward, with later rewards counting a bit less ({{b:Ch.3 p.44-45}}):</p>
<div class="formula"><span class="ft">Return (book Ch.3 p.44)</span>
G<sub>t</sub> = r<sub>t+1</sub> + &gamma; r<sub>t+2</sub> + &gamma;<sup>2</sup> r<sub>t+3</sub> + &hellip; &nbsp;&nbsp;with &gamma; = 0.99</div>
<p>With &gamma; = 0.99 a reward 100 steps ahead (100 x 5 s &asymp; 8 minutes) still counts about 37 %. That is the time scale on which a queue forms and clears, so it is the right horizon.</p>
<div class="box warn"><span class="t">Where we differ from the book</span>
<p>The book calls a discount factor "optimal" between 0.2 and 0.8 ({{b:Ch.3 p.45}}); that is a rule of thumb for its small examples where reward comes quickly. In traffic, the effect of a decision is spread over minutes, so such a small &gamma; would make the agent short-sighted (0.8<sup>10</sup> &asymp; 0.1: it would ignore anything beyond ~1 minute). 0.99 is also the usual default for PPO.</p></div>
<div class="box code"><span class="t">Where in the code</span>
<p>Loop and one environment step: {{c:src/environment/decentralized_sumo_env.py:191}} - runs 5 simulated seconds through the safety layer, then builds new states and rewards for all 98 junctions. Episode length and decision interval: {{c:configs/environment.yaml:6}}, {{c:configs/environment.yaml:9}}. Fake-SUMO unit tests: {{c:tests/test_envs_fake_traci.py:1}}.</p></div>
</div>

<!-- ===================================================== 4 STATE ===================================================== -->
<div class="sec">
<h2>4 &nbsp;State: what each junction sees (22 numbers)</h2>
<div class="box plain"><span class="t">In plain words</span>
<p>Imagine a police officer at one junction. To decide "keep green or switch?" they need: <i>which road has green now, for how long, and how many cars are queuing on each road - including the road behind the short stretch just before the line, cars still waiting to enter the city, and the queue at the next junctions up and down the road.</i> That is exactly the 22 numbers, each squashed into the range &minus;1 to +1 so the network can digest them.</p></div>

