<div style="width:96pt;background:#cfe0f7"></div><div style="width:16pt;background:#f6d9c8"></div><div style="width:32pt;background:#f1e3a8"></div>
<div style="width:96pt;background:#c9ebdd"></div><div style="width:16pt;background:#e6c7e9"></div><div style="width:16pt;background:#e6c7e9"></div><div style="width:16pt;background:#d9d8d3"></div>
<div style="width:64pt;background:#d6d2f4"></div></div>
<div class="strip-l">
<div style="width:96pt">1-6 active green</div><div style="width:16pt">7</div><div style="width:32pt">8-9</div>
<div style="width:96pt">10-15 demand per phase</div><div style="width:16pt">16</div><div style="width:16pt">17</div><div style="width:16pt">18</div><div style="width:64pt">19-22 neighbours</div></div>

<table>
<tr><th style="width:9%">Nos.</th><th style="width:25%">What</th><th>How it is computed / why it is there</th></tr>
<tr><td>1-6</td><td>Which green is on</td><td>One-hot over up to 6 green "slots" (a junction has 2-6 greens). Tells the network which of its own roads currently moves.</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>In transition?</td><td>1 while yellow / all-red is running (nothing can be changed then).</td></tr>
<tr><td>8-9</td><td>Time in this green</td><td>elapsed &divide; <i>that phase's own minimum green</i>, and elapsed &divide; its maximum. Scaled into [0,1].</td></tr>
<tr><td>10-15</td><td>Demand per green phase</td><td><i>d<sub>i</sub></i> = &sum; over movements that green releases of [ halted cars on the lane + halted cars on its feeder lanes + share of waiting-outside cars &minus; halted cars on the lane it flows into ]; squashed <i>d</i>/(|<i>d</i>|+8).</td></tr>
<tr><td>16</td><td>Worst queue</td><td>longest queue on the junction's own lanes, q/(q+8).</td></tr>
<tr><td>17</td><td>Cars waiting outside</td><td>cars scheduled to enter at this junction but not yet inserted, p/(p+20).</td></tr>
<tr><td>18</td><td>Number of greens</td><td>n<sub>green</sub> &divide; 6 - tells the shared network what kind of junction it is.</td></tr>
<tr><td>19-22</td><td>Neighbours</td><td>queue of the 2 strongest road-connected junctions upstream and 2 downstream (0 if none).</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="formula"><span class="ft">Squashing function (used for 10-22)</span>
squash(<i>x</i>) = <i>x</i> / (|<i>x</i>| + <i>k</i>) &nbsp;&nbsp; e.g. with <i>k</i> = 8: &nbsp; 0 &rarr; 0 &nbsp;&nbsp; 8 cars &rarr; 0.50 &nbsp;&nbsp; 24 cars &rarr; 0.75 &nbsp;&nbsp; &minus;8 &rarr; &minus;0.50<br>
Any number of cars maps smoothly into (&minus;1, 1); small queues still change the input visibly, huge queues do not saturate abruptly.</div>

<h3>Three design decisions that mattered</h3>
<ol>
<li><b>Timing relative to the phase's own minimum and maximum (inputs 8-9).</b> We measured which inputs the winning baseline effectively uses: predicting <i>when actuated control leaves a green</i> from our old 18 inputs gave an AUC of only 0.757 (0.5 = guessing); adding "elapsed &divide; own minimum green" lifted it to <b>0.977</b>. The old agent was blind to the one thing the baseline reacts to. (Analysis: {{c:artifacts/eda/observation_relevance/REPORT.md:35}})</li>
<li><b>Feeder lanes (reach 300 m).</b> Some stop-line lanes are only ~20 m long (about 2 cars) while 60 cars wait on the unsignalised road behind them. The old agent saw "2 cars". We now walk upstream along unsignalised roads up to 300 m and add the halted cars. ({{c:src/environment/local_observation.py:191}}, setting <code>queue_reach_m</code> in {{c:configs/environment.yaml:14}})</li>
<li><b>Cars waiting outside the network (input 17, and added to demand in stage 2).</b> A jammed entry road leaves cars queuing <i>outside</i> the simulated network, where the agent saw nothing. We count them in input 17 and, in stage 2, add them to the demand of the lane they will queue at (<code>pending_in_demand</code>, {{c:configs/environment_pending.yaml:16}}).</li>
</ol>
<div class="box book"><span class="t">Book link</span>
<p>The book's Markov property ({{b:Ch.3 p.41}}) requires the state to contain what the future depends on. Because a junction cannot see everything ({{b:Ch.1 p.13}}), these three additions are our way of putting the most decision-relevant hidden facts into the state.</p></div>
<div class="box code"><span class="t">Where in the code</span>
<ul>
<li>Layout and names: {{c:src/environment/local_observation.py:33}} (<code>OBS_SIZE</code>, <code>OBS_NAMES</code>); builder: {{c:src/environment/local_observation.py:151}} (<code>build_observation</code>); per-phase demand: {{c:src/environment/local_observation.py:77}}.</li>
<li>Environment side (queue reach, feeders, road neighbours): {{c:src/environment/decentralized_sumo_env.py:302}}, {{c:src/environment/decentralized_sumo_env.py:323}}, {{c:src/environment/decentralized_sumo_env.py:340}}. Tests: {{c:tests/test_observation.py:1}}.</li>
</ul></div>
</div>

<!-- ===================================================== 5 ACTION ===================================================== -->
<div>
<h2>5 &nbsp;Action: keep or advance - behind a safety layer</h2>
<div class="box plain"><span class="t">In plain words</span>
<p>The network can only ask one of two things every 5 seconds: "stay on this green" (0) or "move on to the next green" (1). It can never switch a light directly - a safety layer (like a traffic engineer's rule book) translates the request and refuses unsafe ones.</p></div>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:30%">Safety rule</th><th>What happens</th><th style="width:22%">Code</th></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum green</td><td>A request to advance is <b>ignored</b> until the current green has run for its own minimum duration.</td><td>{{c:src/environment/safety.py:172}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maximum green</td><td>If a green reaches its maximum the layer <b>forces</b> the advance, whatever the network says.</td><td>{{c:src/environment/safety.py:161}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yellow / all-red</td><td>Every change walks through the signal program's own clearance phases (3 s yellow, 1 s all-red where defined). The network cannot skip them.</td><td>{{c:src/environment/safety.py:106}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Own cycle only</td><td>"Advance" always goes to the junction's <i>next green in its own program</i> - never to an arbitrary phase.</td><td>{{c:src/scenario/tls_metadata.py:100}}</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Per-phase minimum and maximum come from the junction's own authored program when it has them (<code>_green_bounds</code>, {{c:src/environment/safety.py:205}}); the configured 5 s / 60 s are only fallbacks. Unit and fuzz tests ({{c:tests/test_safety_layer.py:1}}, {{c:tests/test_safety_property.py:1}}) exercise these rules on every real junction.</p>
<div class="box book"><span class="t">Book link</span>
<p>A discrete action space, where the agent picks one of a few actions, is the setting of {{b:Ch.1 p.11}} and of the book's policy-gradient chapter ({{b:Ch.11 p.225}}). Our two-action choice is the simplest case: the policy outputs one probability, p(advance).</p></div>
<div class="box warn"><span class="t">Honest note</span>
<p>The network changes phases more often than the baseline: about 17,600 (A) and 18,600 (B) phase changes per hour network-wide against 14,600 for actuated control. Because every change passes through yellow, this has a cost that the reward charges for (section 6), and the measured results already include it.</p></div>
</div>

<!-- ===================================================== 6 REWARD ===================================================== -->
<div>
<h2>6 &nbsp;Reward: what the network is paid for</h2>
<div class="box book"><span class="t">What the book says</span>
<p>The reward is the feedback signal; the agent's objective is to maximise the return, the discounted sum of rewards ({{b:Ch.3 p.44}}). The book also notes that rewards are often <b>clipped</b> to a small range to keep learning stable ({{b:Ch.8 p.174}}).</p></div>
<p>Every junction, every 5 seconds, pays a <b>cost</b> that is the weighted sum of five things; the reward is its negative:</p>
<div class="formula"><span class="ft">Reward of one junction for one step</span>
<i>r</i> = &minus; 0.1 &times; min( &nbsp;<i>Q</i> + 2<i>D</i> + <i>S</i> + 0.5 <i>P</i><sub>own</sub> + 0.5 <i>P</i><sub>all</sub> / 98 &nbsp;,&nbsp; 200 )</div>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:10%">Symbol</th><th style="width:12%">Weight</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><i>Q</i></td><td>1</td><td>cars standing still on this junction's incoming lanes (including the feeder roads behind them).</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>D</i></td><td>2</td><td>average cars standing per lane on the roads this junction <i>feeds into</i> - so pushing a queue onto a full road is punished (spill-back).</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>S</i></td><td>1</td><td>1 if the junction changed phase in this step, else 0 - a small price on needless switching.</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>P</i><sub>own</sub></td><td>0.5</td><td>cars waiting outside the network whose first signal is this junction.</td></tr>
<tr><td><i>P</i><sub>all</sub> / 98</td><td>0.5</td><td>all cars waiting outside, divided over the 98 junctions - a shared term so every junction cares that the city can take cars in.</td></tr>
<tr><td>min(&middot;, 200), 0.1</td><td>-</td><td>the cost is capped at 200 (outlier protection) and scaled by 0.1 so rewards sit in roughly [&minus;20, 0] ({{b:Ch.8 p.174}}).</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="box plain"><span class="t">Worked example</span>
<p>A junction with 6 standing cars, 2 per lane on the road it feeds, no switch, 4 cars waiting outside for it and 300 waiting outside in total:<br>
cost = 6 + 2&middot;2 + 0 + 0.5&middot;4 + 0.5&middot;300/98 = 6 + 4 + 0 + 2 + 1.53 = 13.53 &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; reward = &minus;0.1 &times; 13.53 = <b>&minus;1.35</b>. If the next step has 3 standing cars instead of 6, cost falls to 10.5 and the reward rises to &minus;1.05 - the network has been paid for the improvement.</p></div>
<h3>Why a <i>level</i>, not a <i>change</i></h3>
<p>Earlier versions rewarded the <i>change</i> in queue from step to step. That can be gamed: wait while the queue grows (small penalties), release it all at once (one big reward). Paying on the <b>level</b> of waiting makes the total return equal to minus the total vehicle-delay, which has no such loophole. The test {{c:tests/test_reward.py:1}} pins this behaviour.</p>
<div class="box code"><span class="t">Where in the code</span>
<p>Weights: {{c:src/environment/local_reward.py:28}} (<code>LocalRewardConfig</code>); formula: {{c:src/environment/local_reward.py:85}} (<code>compute</code>); clip: {{c:src/environment/local_reward.py:97}}; outside-cars bookkeeping: {{c:src/environment/local_reward.py:57}}. Values come from <code>reward:</code> in {{c:configs/environment.yaml:29}}.</p></div>
</div>

<!-- ===================================================== 7 PPO ===================================================== -->
<div class="sec">
<h2>7 &nbsp;The learning algorithm: PPO, used as IPPO</h2>
<h3>7.1 &nbsp;The ladder of ideas (all in the book)</h3>
<div class="chain">
<div><b>Policy gradient</b>Nudge the policy so actions that gave a high return become more likely.<br>{{b:Ch.11 p.225-226}}</div><div class="ar">&rarr;</div>
<div><b>Actor-critic</b>A second network (critic) predicts value; the actor learns from the <i>advantage</i> A = Q &minus; V: "better or worse than expected?"<br>{{b:Ch.10 p.209-211}}</div><div class="ar">&rarr;</div>
<div><b>TRPO</b>Limit how far one update may move the policy, or learning collapses.<br>{{b:Ch.11 p.240}}</div><div class="ar">&rarr;</div>
<div><b>PPO</b>Same safety idea, but simply <i>clip</i> the update. Easy and stable.<br>{{b:Ch.11 p.245-247}}</div></div>

<h3>7.2 &nbsp;The PPO objective</h3>
<div class="formula"><span class="ft">PPO clipped objective (book Ch.11 p.245-247)</span>
<i>r</i><sub>t</sub>(&theta;) = &pi;<sub>&theta;</sub>(a<sub>t</sub>|s<sub>t</sub>) / &pi;<sub>old</sub>(a<sub>t</sub>|s<sub>t</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp;(how much more likely the new policy makes the action)<br>
<i>L</i><sup>CLIP</sup> = E[ min( <i>r</i><sub>t</sub> &middot; A<sub>t</sub> , clip(<i>r</i><sub>t</sub>, 1&minus;&epsilon;, 1+&epsilon;) &middot; A<sub>t</sub> ) ] &nbsp;&nbsp;with &epsilon; = 0.2<br>
<i>L</i> = &minus;<i>L</i><sup>CLIP</sup> + c<sub>1</sub> &middot; (V<sub>&theta;</sub>(s<sub>t</sub>) &minus; G<sub>t</sub>)<sup>2</sup> &minus; c<sub>2</sub> &middot; H[&pi;<sub>&theta;</sub>] &nbsp;&nbsp;with c<sub>1</sub> = 0.5, c<sub>2</sub> = 0.01</div>
<div class="fig"><img src="fig/fig_ppo_clip.png" width="92%"><div class="cap">Figure 2 - Why clipping keeps updates small. Left: for a good action (A &gt; 0) the gain stops growing once the action is 20 % more likely than before. Right: for a bad action the push-down also stops at 0.8. Inside the blue band the update behaves normally. (Same picture as the book's {{b:Ch.11 p.246}}.)</div></div>
<div class="box plain"><span class="t">In plain words</span>
<p>After the network has played some traffic, we look back: <i>"in this situation I advanced the green and the outcome was better than the critic expected."</i> We then make that choice a little more likely - but never by more than 20 % in one update, so one lucky batch cannot wreck what was already learnt. The third term (entropy, {{b:Ch.10 p.211}}) rewards staying a bit random so the network keeps exploring other options; the book's epsilon-greedy ({{b:Ch.6 p.114}}) has the same aim, but a policy-gradient method explores by <i>sampling</i> from its own probabilities instead.</p></div>

<div class="keep"><h3>7.3 &nbsp;The network and the settings</h3>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:27%">Setting</th><th style="width:15%">Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Network (actor and critic each)</td><td>22 &rarr; 256 &rarr; 256 &rarr; out, tanh</td><td>Two hidden layers of 256 units; tanh activation ({{b:Ch.7 p.132}}). {{c:configs/ippo.yaml:24}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Optimiser, learning rate</td><td>Adam, 3e-4</td><td>Gradient descent with adaptive step sizes ({{b:Ch.7 p.136}}). {{c:configs/ippo.yaml:20}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Discount &gamma;</td><td>0.99</td><td>Section 3. {{c:configs/ippo.yaml:12}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>GAE &lambda;</td><td>0.95</td><td>Advantage smoothing, section 7.4. {{c:configs/ippo.yaml:13}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clip range &epsilon;</td><td>0.2</td><td>{{c:configs/ippo.yaml:15}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Value coef. c<sub>1</sub> / entropy coef. c<sub>2</sub></td><td>0.5 / 0.01</td><td>{{c:configs/ippo.yaml:17}}, {{c:configs/ippo.yaml:16}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Samples per update</td><td>105,840</td><td>180 steps &times; 98 junctions &times; 6 simulations. {{c:configs/ippo.yaml:7}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mini-batches, epochs</td><td>8 of 13,230; 10 epochs</td><td>Each batch of experience is reused 10 times. {{c:configs/ippo.yaml:8}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Updates</td><td>100 (+10 or +20)</td><td>10,584,000 steps in stage 1 ({{c:configs/ippo.yaml:6}}); stage 2 in section 8.</td></tr>
</table></div>

<h3>7.4 &nbsp;Three things that are <u>not</u> in the book</h3>
<div class="box warn"><span class="t">Beyond the book</span>
<ol>
<li><b>One network for every junction - IPPO (parameter sharing).</b> "Independent PPO" means each junction decides on its own from its own observation, but all junctions use <i>the same weights</i>, and every junction's experience trains them. Per update the one network learns from 98 junctions &times; 6 simulations = <b>588 simultaneous "players"</b>, i.e. 98 times more experience per simulated hour than a network for one junction. It works because junctions play the same role; the active-slot and number-of-greens inputs let the network tell junction types apart. (The book's A3C {{b:Ch.10 p.209}} also gathers experience from many parallel copies of the environment; ours is the synchronous variant - all 6 simulations finish a batch, then one update runs.)</li>
<li><b>Generalised advantage estimation (GAE).</b> The book's advantage is "return &minus; value" ({{b:Ch.10 p.209}}). GAE averages short and long look-aheads with weight &lambda; = 0.95: less noise than waiting for the full return, less bias than trusting the critic after one step. Standard in modern PPO libraries (Stable-Baselines3 here).</li>
<li><b>Split gradient clipping</b> - our own fix, next section.</li>
</ol></div>
<div class="box code"><span class="t">Where in the code</span>
<p>Parallel environments: {{c:src/agents/ippo_agent.py:37}} (<code>make_ippo_env</code>). Model construction with every hyper-parameter above: {{c:src/agents/ippo_agent.py:221}} (<code>build_model</code>), learning loop {{c:src/agents/ippo_agent.py:292}} (<code>_run_learn</code>), resume after interruption {{c:src/agents/ippo_agent.py:270}}. Entry points: {{c:scripts/run_ippo_training.py:33}} (stage 1), {{c:scripts/finetune_ippo.py:18}} (stage 2). The PPO implementation itself is Stable-Baselines3's; the contract that a model must be evaluated under the settings it was trained with is {{c:src/agents/model_contract.py:115}}.</p></div>
</div>

<!-- ===================================================== 8 WHAT WE CHANGED ===================================================== -->
<div class="sec">
<h2>8 &nbsp;What we changed to beat the baseline - five fixes in plain English</h2>
<p>The first versions of the agent <i>lost</i> clearly to the baseline (lag 363-476 s against 277 s). We went looking for the reasons one by one; each row is a root cause that was found with evidence and fixed.</p>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:4%">#</th><th style="width:27%">What was wrong</th><th style="width:43%">Fix (plain English)</th><th>Code</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td><b>The network was barely learning.</b> The policy moved ~0 per update ("clip fraction" = 0).</td><td>PPO clips the gradient to keep updates stable - but the actor and critic were clipped <i>together</i>. The critic's gradient was ~3,830, the actor's only ~0.19, so the common shrink factor made the actor's steps ~7,600 times smaller. <b>Fix: clip them separately.</b> (Figure 3)</td><td>{{c:src/agents/split_clip.py:28}}<br>{{c:tests/test_split_clip.py:1}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td><b>A see-saw reward.</b> It paid for <i>changes</i> in queue.</td><td>Paying for change can be gamed (let it grow, then release). <b>Fix: pay for the level</b> of waiting, so return = minus total delay.</td><td>{{c:src/environment/local_reward.py:85}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td><b>Dead or stuck inputs, and blind to timing.</b> Two inputs sat at their maximum 81 % and 90 % of the hour; the signal the baseline uses (time in phase vs its minimum) was missing.</td><td><b>Fix: a new, smaller 22-number observation</b> with the per-phase timing ratios and a demand number per green (section 4).</td><td>{{c:src/environment/local_observation.py:151}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td><b>Blind behind short lanes.</b> A 20 m stop-line lane hid 60 queued cars.</td><td><b>Fix: look up to 300 m upstream</b> and count halted cars on the unsignalised feeder roads.</td><td>{{c:src/environment/local_observation.py:191}}</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td><b>Blind to cars waiting <u>outside</u> the network.</b> After stages 1-4 the model was already ~26 s of lag ahead but lost ~100 more cars at the network entrance than the baseline.</td><td><b>Fix, stage 2:</b> add outside-waiting cars to the demand of the lane they will queue at, then fine-tune the trained network for 10 (A) or 20 (B) more updates.</td><td>{{c:configs/environment_pending.yaml:16}}<br>{{c:scripts/finetune_ippo.py:18}}</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="fig"><img src="fig/fig_gradnorm.png" width="68%"><div class="cap">Figure 3 - Fix 1. Typical gradient sizes during stage-1 training. With one shared limit of 0.5 the critic's huge gradient sets the shrink factor for both networks, so the actor's update was reduced about 7,600x. With separate limits each network is clipped on its own.</div></div>
<div class="box code"><span class="t">How split clipping works (about 40 lines)</span>
<p>PyTorch's <code>clip_grad_norm_</code> is temporarily replaced by a version that clips the actor's parameters and the critic's parameters as two groups ({{c:src/agents/split_clip.py:28}}); <code>SplitClipPPO</code> ({{c:src/agents/split_clip.py:62}}) switches it on during <code>train()</code> and logs both norms. It is enabled by <code>split_clip: true</code> in {{c:configs/ippo.yaml:19}}.</p></div>
<div class="box warn"><span class="t">Beyond the book</span>
<p>Gradient clipping against "exploding gradients" is mentioned in the book only in passing (e.g. {{b:Ch.7 p.149}}); clipping <i>two networks separately inside PPO</i> is our own engineering fix, validated by the change in learning behaviour and by the final results, not by a reference.</p></div>
<h3>The two stages of training</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Stage 1</b> - from scratch, 100 PPO updates (10,584,000 steps, about 7 hours), recipe {{c:configs/ippo.yaml:1}} + {{c:configs/environment.yaml:1}}.</li>
<li><b>Stage 2</b> - continue the stage-1 network with <code>pending_in_demand: true</code> ({{c:configs/ippo_finetune.yaml:1}} + {{c:configs/environment_pending.yaml:1}}). The input size is unchanged, so the trained weights are reused; only what feeds the demand numbers changes. Model A: +10 updates (seed 42). Model B: +20 updates (seed 1042).</li>
</ul>
</div>

<!-- ===================================================== 9 METRICS ===================================================== -->
<div>
<h2>9 &nbsp;How we measure - a fair test</h2>
<h3>9.1 &nbsp;Same traffic for model and baseline</h3>
<p>SUMO has randomness (exact departure moments, driver behaviour). A <b>seed</b> fixes that randomness. For each of <b>10 seeds</b> (7, 11, 19, 23, 31, 42, 57, 91, 123, 777) we run baseline, fixed-time, model A and model B on <i>exactly the same traffic</i> and compare run against run ("paired by seed"). 4 controllers &times; 10 seeds = 40 one-hour simulations. The seeds used while training were all above 100,000 (each parallel training worker adds 100,000 &times; its number to the seed, {{c:src/environment/sumo_session.py:78}}), so none of the ten test seeds was ever seen in training.</p>
<h3>9.2 &nbsp;The main number: lag per scheduled car</h3>
<div class="formula"><span class="ft">Lag of one scheduled car (W = 08:00)</span>
lag = ( W &minus; <i>t</i><sub>ideal</sub> )<sup>+</sup> &minus; ( W &minus; <i>t</i><sub>arrival</sub> )<sup>+</sup> &nbsp;&nbsp; with <i>t</i><sub>arrival</sub> := W if the car never finished or never entered<br>
<b>lag per scheduled car</b> = average of lag over <b>all</b> ~15,000 scheduled cars</div>
<div class="box plain"><span class="t">Worked example</span>
<p>A car would finish at 07:30 on an empty road (<i>t</i><sub>ideal</sub>). Case 1: it really finishes at 07:42 &rarr; lag = 30 min &minus; 18 min = <b>12 min</b>. Case 2: it never finishes (still queuing at 08:00, or stuck outside) &rarr; lag = 30 min &minus; 0 = <b>30 min</b>: it is charged for every minute until the window closed. So a controller cannot look good by letting some cars simply disappear from the statistics - the usual weakness of "mean trip time of the cars that finished".</p></div>
<h3>9.3 &nbsp;The other measures</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Never inserted</b> - scheduled cars that could not enter the network because the entry road was full. <b>Depart delay</b> - waiting time at the entrance for those that did enter.</li>
<li><b>Backlog</b> - cars still in the network (or waiting outside) at 08:00. <b>Due completion</b> - share of cars that should have finished by 08:00 that did. <b>Overdue cars</b> - how many were due but not finished.</li>
<li><b>Waiting time, queue, speed, time loss, trip duration, throughput, trips completed</b> - the classic traffic measures. <b>Teleports</b> - SUMO removes cars stuck for too long: a sign of gridlock.</li>
</ul>
<h3>9.4 &nbsp;When do we say "beaten"?</h3>
<p>We take the 10 per-seed differences (model &minus; baseline) and require <b>both</b>: (i) the 95 % confidence interval of the mean difference excludes zero on the good side, and (ii) the exact <b>sign test</b> is significant - the model wins on at least 9 of 10 seeds (chance of 10 / 10 by luck = 2 &times; 0.5<sup>10</sup> = 0.002). One strange seed therefore cannot carry a verdict. The table in section 10 shows the verdict for each measure; weaker outcomes are named honestly ("better, not significant", "level").</p>
<div class="box code"><span class="t">Where in the code</span>
<ul>
<li>Scoring of one run (lag formula at line 420): {{c:src/evaluation/drain_metrics.py:387}} (<code>score_run</code>); scheduled demand and the empty-road ideal time: {{c:src/evaluation/drain_metrics.py:299}}.</li>
<li>Running a controller on a seed: {{c:src/evaluation/evaluate.py:77}}; command-line: {{c:scripts/evaluate.py:23}}.</li>
<li>Paired statistics and verdict rule: {{c:scripts/report_eval.py:67}}, verdict at {{c:scripts/report_eval.py:87}}; interval helpers {{c:src/evaluation/statistics.py:1}}. The full result table is in {{c:docs/TEST_REPORT_10_SEEDS.md:1}}.</li>
</ul></div>
</div>

