<!-- ===================================================== 11 LIMITS ===================================================== -->
<div>
<h2>11 &nbsp;Honest limits - what this does <u>not</u> show</h2>
<div class="box warn"><span class="t">Please read before quoting a result</span>
<ul>
<li><b>"Cars never inserted" is not won.</b> A first 3-seed test suggested a win (473 vs 539); the 10-seed test shows the baseline was unusually high on those three seeds. The honest statement: the stage-2 fix <i>closed</i> the entrance gap (stage 1 lost ~100 more cars than the baseline) but did not open a lead.</li>
<li><b>Teleports</b> are too noisy (6 / 10 seeds) to claim in either direction.</li>
<li><b>One scenario.</b> Training and test use the same network and the same demand pattern (07:00-08:00 InTAS); seeds only change the random details. The 08:00 and 17:00 windows (one seed, model A) are a first check on other times of day; a different city or real traffic is untested.</li>
<li><b>Sensing is generous.</b> The agent reads exact halted-car counts per lane, including cars still waiting outside. Real junctions have detectors with limited range and noise; degraded sensing was not tested.</li>
<li><b>Choices made after seeing data.</b> Model B received a second fine-tune block (10 &rarr; 20 updates) after a 3-seed result, and the strict "beaten" rule was fixed after the first results (it is stricter than the first rule). Both are disclosed; neither was selected on the 10-seed test set.</li>
<li><b>More phase changes</b> than the baseline (section 5) - the results already include this cost, but wear and comfort are not modelled.</li>
</ul></div>

