<h2 style="margin-top:14pt">13 &nbsp;One-paragraph summary for the viva</h2>
<div class="box plain"><span class="t">If asked "what did you do?"</span>
<p>We modelled morning traffic in Ingolstadt as a reinforcement-learning problem: one shared neural network, copied to all 98 junctions, sees 22 numbers about its junction and chooses every 5 seconds to keep or advance the green, behind a safety layer; it is paid minus the cars waiting. We trained it with PPO (book Ch.11), found why the first versions failed - the actor's learning signal was being squashed by the critic's gradient, the reward could be gamed, the inputs were blind to the baseline's timing logic, to long queues behind short lanes and to cars waiting outside - fixed each, and then tested on 10 seeds of identical traffic against SUMO's own actuated control. The trained network is significantly better on 12 (and 11) of 14 measures - e.g. 41 % less waiting, about 200-260 more completed trips per hour - and worse on none; it does not reduce the number of cars blocked at the network entrance, and has only been tested on this one scenario.</p></div>
</div>

